## Lesson 41 – Finite State Fun

Welcome to Week 6, where games stop being a single loop and start behaving like systems. Arcade cabinets lived on **finite state machines**: title attract mode, gameplay, pause, game over. By the end of this lesson you’ll guide the C64 through those beats with one controlling variable and a tidy loop.

> [📷 suggested: screenshot swapping between title, play HUD, and game-over screens]

---

### The One-Minute Tour

- A *state* is just a number describing what the program should do right now.
- `ON … GOSUB` routes control to the right handler without messy `GOTO` chains.
- Keep a `lastState` variable so you only redraw the screen when something changes.

---

### Example Program

```basic
NEW
10 REM *** FINITE STATE LOOP ***
20 PRINT CHR$(147)
30 STATE=0:LAST=-1:LIVES=3:SCORE=0:KEY$=""
40 TI$="000000"
50 GET K$:IF K$<>"" THEN KEY$=K$
60 IF STATE<>LAST THEN GOSUB 400
70 ON STATE+1 GOSUB 500,600,700
80 LAST=STATE
90 GOTO 50
400 REM *** STATE DRAWER ***
410 IF STATE=0 THEN GOSUB 900
420 IF STATE=1 THEN GOSUB 920
430 IF STATE=2 THEN GOSUB 940
440 RETURN
500 REM *** TITLE STATE ***
510 IF KEY$=" " THEN STATE=1:KEY$="":LIVES=3:SCORE=0:TI$="000000"
520 IF KEY$="Q" THEN END
530 KEY$="":RETURN
600 REM *** PLAY STATE ***
610 IF TI>60 THEN SCORE=SCORE+10:TI$="000000"
620 IF KEY$="H" THEN LIVES=LIVES-1:KEY$=""
630 IF KEY$="P" THEN STATE=0:KEY$="":RETURN
640 IF LIVES<0 THEN STATE=2:RETURN
650 PRINT CHR$(19);"SCORE:";SCORE;"  LIVES:";LIVES;"   "
660 KEY$="":RETURN
700 REM *** GAME OVER STATE ***
710 IF KEY$="R" THEN STATE=0:KEY$=""
720 IF KEY$="Q" THEN END
730 KEY$="":RETURN
900 PRINT CHR$(147)
910 PRINT "*** STARFIELD COMMAND ***";:PRINT:PRINT "SPACE = LAUNCH  Q = QUIT"
915 PRINT "TIP: PRESS H DURING PLAY TO LOSE A LIFE"
916 PRINT "PRESS SPACE TO START"
917 RETURN
920 PRINT CHR$(147)
930 PRINT "MISSION ACTIVE";:PRINT:PRINT "H = HAZARD  P = PAUSE"
931 PRINT "SURVIVE AND SCORE!"
932 RETURN
940 PRINT CHR$(147)
950 PRINT "MISSION FAILED";:PRINT:PRINT "R = RETRY  Q = QUIT"
951 PRINT "YOUR SCORE:";SCORE
952 RETURN
```

Sample output (title state):

```
*** STARFIELD COMMAND ***

SPACE = LAUNCH  Q = QUIT
TIP: PRESS H DURING PLAY TO LOSE A LIFE
PRESS SPACE TO START
```

Line 30 sets up the state machine and zeroes the shared variables. The main loop (50–90) polls input, redraws the screen only when the state changes (`STATE<>LAST`), and hands control to the right handler with `ON STATE+1 GOSUB 2000,3000,4000`. Title, play, and game-over logic each live in small subroutines, so you can add or remove states without tearing up the loop.

> **Tip:** Keep every state self-contained. If a state needs setup, do it in the “state drawer” block so the main loop stays identical no matter how many states you add.

---

### Experiment Section

- Add a pause state: when the player presses `P`, set `STATE=3` and draw a pause overlay.
- Swap the simple score timer for your Scroll Runner timer from Week 5 to prove states work with fixed steps.
- Flash the background when `STATE` switches to game over—instant dramatic feedback.
- Store state names in an array: `STATE$()` keeps your HUD text tidy.

> [🎥 suggested: clip showing transitions from title → play → game over → retry]

---

### Concept Expansion

Finite states give structure to everything we build this week. Enemies can switch from patrolling to chasing, projectiles swap between armed and exploding, power-ups cycle through availability. Once you trust the pattern you can grow the machine as wide as you like without getting lost in `GOTO` spaghetti.

---

### Game Integration

Use states to gate player control:

```basic
IF STATE=1 THEN GOSUB 3000
IF STATE=2 THEN GOSUB 4000
```

Keep the input routine at line `3000` and the restart prompt at `4000`. This way the main loop never wonders whether the player should move—it simply delegates based on the current mode.

